# Build context filter for BOTH images (`apps/api/Dockerfile` and
# `apps/web/Dockerfile` build from the repo ROOT — the API depends on the
# `@flowboard/shared` workspace package, so neither can build from its own
# directory).
#
# Three jobs, in order of how much they matter:
#
# 1. KEEP THE HOST'S node_modules OUT. It holds workspace SYMLINKS and
#    platform-specific binaries (esbuild, rollup) built for the host OS.
#    Copying it over a container install is how `tsup: MODULE_NOT_FOUND` and
#    "you installed esbuild for another platform" happen. This is a
#    correctness rule, not an optimisation.
# 2. KEEP SECRETS OUT. `.env` holds real credentials on a developer machine and
#    would otherwise be baked into an image layer forever. The API image also
#    sets `NODE_ENV=production` in its release stage, and `config/env.ts` loads
#    dotenv WITHOUT overriding an already-set variable — so even if a `.env`
#    reached the image it could not downgrade the runtime to development. Two
#    independent guards, because that one is worth two.
# 3. Keep the context small — every ignored path is bytes not sent to the
#    daemon and a layer not invalidated by an unrelated edit.
#
# NOT IGNORED, deliberately:
#   - `apps/api/drizzle/**` — the generated migrations. `db:migrate` runs from
#     the image, so a build that dropped them would produce an API that cannot
#     provision its own database.
#   - `.npmrc` — carries `node-linker=hoisted`; every install needs it.
#   - `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, every `package.json`.
#
# PATTERNS ARE ROOT-ANCHORED. Unlike `.gitignore`, a bare `dist` here matches
# ONLY `./dist` — not `apps/api/dist`. That is why every nested-able entry is
# written twice, once bare and once as `**/…`. Verified by copying the context
# into a scratch image and asserting on what landed; a silent leak here is a
# workspace symlink or a stale build in a production image.

# ── Dependencies ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules
**/node_modules
.pnpm-store

# ── Build outputs (the image builds its own) ─────────────────────────────────
dist
**/dist
build
**/build
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# ── Tooling caches ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.turbo
**/.turbo
.vitest
**/.vitest

# ── VCS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github

# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── Test / coverage artifacts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
coverage
**/coverage
test-results
**/test-results
playwright-report
**/playwright-report
blob-report
**/blob-report
.playwright
**/.playwright
**/playwright/.cache
# NOT `e2e/` itself: both Dockerfiles `COPY e2e/package.json`, because
# `--frozen-lockfile` compares the lockfile's importers against the workspace
# members it can find and reads a missing one as a stale lockfile.
e2e/tests
e2e/playwright.config.ts

# ── Documentation (no build step reads a .md) ────────────────────────────────
# `.agents/` in particular is a large tree that changes constantly and would
# invalidate the build context on every doc edit.
.agents
docs
**/*.md

# ── Docker's own files (an image never needs to build itself) ────────────────
Dockerfile
**/Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.dev.yml

# ── Editor / OS noise ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.idea
.vscode
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
*.log
**/*.log
